      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "92b284f1-90e3-53f2-b8ce-4e16c80ca892",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Medhatithi Gautama, Pāṇini, Gotama, Nagarjuna, Akṣapāda Gautama\n- Organizations: Vaisheshika school, Nyaya school\n- Products: Nyāya Sūtras\n- Concepts: Indian logic, anviksiki, atomism, inference, tetralemma, reason, logic, epistemology, metaphysics\n\nFacts:\n- Indian logic developed from the anviksiki of Medhatithi Gautama around the 7th century BCE.\n- Indian logic developed from the Sanskrit grammar rules of Pāṇini around the 5th century BCE.\n- Indian logic developed from the Vaisheshika school's analysis of atomism around the 2nd century BCE.\n- Gotama, founder of the Nyaya school of Hindu philosophy, contributed an analysis of inference to Indian logic around the 2nd century.\n- Nagarjuna contributed the tetralemma to Indian logic around the 2nd century CE.\n- The Nyāya Sūtras is an ancient Indian Sanskrit text composed by Akṣapāda Gautama.\n- The Nyāya Sūtras is the foundational text of the Nyaya school of Hindu philosophy.\n- The Nyāya Sūtras consists of five books with two chapters in each book and a total of 528 aphoristic sutras about reason, logic, epistemology, and metaphysics.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "5bb9b98c-fb37-57f6-8925-795d12b98840",
        "text": "Page: Indian logic. Sentence: The development of Indian logic dates back to the \"anviksiki\" of Medhatithi Gautama (c. 7th century BCE) the Sanskrit grammar rules of Pāṇini (c. 5th century BCE); the Vaisheshika school's analysis of atomism (c. 2nd century BCE); the analysis of inference by Gotama (c. 2nd century), founder of the Nyaya school of Hindu philosophy; and the tetralemma of Nagarjuna (c. 2nd century CE).\nPage: Nyāya Sūtras. Sentence: The Nyāya Sūtras is an ancient Indian Sanskrit text composed by Akṣapāda Gautama , and the foundational text of the Nyaya school of Hindu philosophy.\nPage: Nyāya Sūtras. Sentence: The text consists of five books, with two chapters in each book, with a cumulative total of 528 aphoristic sutras, about rules of reason, logic, epistemology and metaphysics.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
